{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"multiple-inheritance","title":"Multiple & Virtual Inheritance","group":"Inheritance & Polymorphism","order":40,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A class can inherit from several bases. That's safe for interfaces, but when two bases share a common ancestor you get the <strong>diamond problem</strong> — solved by <code>virtual</code> inheritance.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-git-merge\"></i>Multiple inheritance</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n\nstruct Printable { virtual void print() const = 0; virtual ~Printable() = default; };\nstruct Serializable { virtual std::string serialize() const = 0; virtual ~Serializable() = default; };\n\nstruct Point : Printable, Serializable {         // implements two interfaces\n    int x = 1, y = 2;\n    void print() const override { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"Point(\" &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; \", \" &lt;&lt; y &lt;&lt; \")\\n\"; }\n    std::string serialize() const override { return std::to_string(x) + \",\" + std::to_string(y); }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Point p;\n    const Printable&amp; pr = p;\n    const Serializable&amp; se = p;\n    pr.print();\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; se.serialize() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Point(1, 2)\n1,2</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-diamond\"></i>The diamond problem</h3>\n<div class=\"flow\"><div class=\"flow-node\"><code>Device</code></div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\"><code>Scanner</code> / <code>Printer</code></div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\"><code>Copier</code></div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n\nstruct Device { int id = 0; };\nstruct Scanner : Device {};\nstruct Printer : Device {};\nstruct Copier : Scanner, Printer {};      // contains TWO Device sub-objects\n\nint main() {\n    Copier c;\n    c.Scanner::id = 1;                     // must disambiguate\n    c.Printer::id = 2;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; c.Scanner::id &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; c.Printer::id &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; sizeof(Copier) / sizeof(int) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    // c.id = 5;                           // error: ambiguous\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1 2 2</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-share\"></i>Virtual inheritance</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n\nstruct Device {\n    int id;\n    explicit Device(int i) : id(i) { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"Device(\" &lt;&lt; i &lt;&lt; \")\\n\"; }\n};\nstruct Scanner : virtual Device { Scanner() : Device(1) {} };   // shared base\nstruct Printer : virtual Device { Printer() : Device(2) {} };\nstruct Copier : Scanner, Printer {\n    Copier() : Device(3) {}               // the MOST-DERIVED class initialises the virtual base\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Copier c;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; c.id &lt;&lt; '\\n';            // one Device, no ambiguity\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Device(3)\n3</code></pre></figure>\n<ul><li>With <code>virtual</code> inheritance there is exactly <strong>one</strong> shared base sub-object.</li><li>The most-derived class constructs the virtual base; the intermediate classes' initialisers for it are ignored.</li><li>Cost: an extra indirection (virtual base offset) to reach the shared base, and more complex object layout.</li></ul>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-cross\"></i>Name clashes between bases</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nstruct Camera { void start() const { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"camera on\\n\"; } };\nstruct Phone  { void start() const { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"phone on\\n\"; } };\nstruct SmartPhone : Camera, Phone {\n    void start() const { Phone::start(); Camera::start(); }   // resolve explicitly\n};\nint main() { SmartPhone{}.start(); }</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>phone on\ncamera on</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Use multiple inheritance of <em>interfaces</em> (pure abstract bases) freely.</p></div><div class=\"callout callout--bad\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Avoid</div><p>Deep hierarchies with multiple <em>stateful</em> bases — prefer composition.</p></div></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the diamond problem and how does C++ solve it?</summary><div><p>When <code>D</code> inherits from <code>B</code> and <code>C</code>, which both inherit from <code>A</code>, <code>D</code> contains two copies of <code>A</code>, so <code>A</code>'s members are ambiguous and the state is duplicated. Declaring <code>B : virtual A</code> and <code>C : virtual A</code> makes them share one <code>A</code> sub-object, which the most-derived class (<code>D</code>) constructs.</p></div></details></section>"}